  <h1 class="story-title">One Company Made 10 Gene Edits to a Pig Kidney. The Other Made 69. Both Just Started Human Trials.</h1>
  <p class="story-deck">United Therapeutics and eGenesis are running the first FDA-approved xenotransplant clinical trials simultaneously — with radically different engineering philosophies. The organ shortage kills 17 Americans a day. The answer might be pork.</p>
  <p class="story-byline">By <strong>Dr. Kenji Watanabe</strong> · Genomics &amp; Biotech · March 13, 2026 · ☕ 6 min read</p>

  <div class="story-body">
    <p>271 days. That's how long Tim Andrews, a 67-year-old from Alabama, lived with a gene-edited pig kidney before his immune system finally overwhelmed it. Surgeons at Massachusetts General Hospital removed the organ in October 2025. He went back on dialysis. But for nine months, a kidney grown in a pig filtered his blood, produced urine, and kept him off the machine.</p>

    <p>Andrews was the second of three patients to receive pig kidneys from eGenesis under compassionate-use authorization. Each kidney carried 69 genomic edits — think of it as 69 individual commits to a porcine codebase: four genes deleted, six human genes inserted, plus 59 retroviral sequences neutralized. It is the most extensively engineered organ ever placed inside a human body.</p>

    <p>Meanwhile, NYU Langone Health transplanted a competing product: United Therapeutics' UKidney, carrying just 10 gene edits. Same organ. Same species. Same disease. Radically different engineering philosophy — the equivalent of a minimal viable product versus a full rewrite.</p>

    <p>Both companies now have FDA-approved clinical trials enrolling patients. The minimalists and the maximalists are about to find out who shipped the right version.</p>

    <h2>The Edit Count Problem</h2>

    <p>Ten edits versus 69 isn't a stylistic disagreement. It's a fundamental bet on what kills a xenotransplant.</p>

    <p>United Therapeutics' approach is surgical. Their 10-gene-edited pig knocks out four porcine genes — three galactose antigens that trigger hyperacute rejection (the immune response that destroyed pig organs within minutes in early experiments) plus one growth-factor gene to prevent the organ from outgrowing its host. Six human complement-regulatory and anti-coagulation genes go in: CD46, CD55, CD59, thrombomodulin, EPCR, and HO-1. Four deletions, six insertions, ship it.</p>

    <p>eGenesis goes further. Their 69-edit pig includes those same immune-evasion modifications but adds an entire second layer: inactivation of all known porcine endogenous retroviruses (PERVs) embedded in the pig genome. PERVs are ancient retroviral sequences baked into porcine DNA — junk code that can't infect pigs, but in vitro studies from the early 2000s showed they could theoretically infect human cells. No PERV transmission has ever been documented in a xenotransplant recipient. But eGenesis, co-founded by CRISPR pioneer George Church at Harvard, decided that "theoretically" wasn't good enough for an organ you're putting inside a living person. So they ran 59 additional CRISPR cuts to silence every known PERV sequence.</p>

    <p>The tradeoff: every additional edit is a potential off-target event. CRISPR doesn't always cut where you tell it to. With 69 edits, the probability of at least one unintended modification rises — it's the biological equivalent of merge conflicts in a massive pull request. eGenesis says whole-genome sequencing of their source pigs shows no off-target damage at detectable levels. Skeptics note that sequencing resolution has limits, and that low-frequency mosaicism — where some cells carry off-target edits and others don't — is fiendishly hard to detect.</p>

    <h2>Scoreboard: Compassionate Use</h2>

    <p>Before either trial launched, both companies had data from compassionate-use transplants — one-off procedures the FDA authorized for patients with no other options.</p>

    <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:1.5em 0">
      <thead><tr style="border-bottom:2px solid var(--border)">
        <th style="text-align:left;padding:8px">Patient</th>
        <th style="text-align:left;padding:8px">Company</th>
        <th style="text-align:right;padding:8px">Edits</th>
        <th style="text-align:left;padding:8px">Outcome</th>
      </tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border)">
          <td style="padding:8px">Rick Slayman, 62</td>
          <td style="padding:8px">eGenesis</td>
          <td style="text-align:right;padding:8px">69</td>
          <td style="padding:8px">March 2024. Died ~2 months post-transplant (unrelated cardiac event)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border)">
          <td style="padding:8px">Tim Andrews, 67</td>
          <td style="padding:8px">eGenesis</td>
          <td style="text-align:right;padding:8px">69</td>
          <td style="padding:8px">Jan 2025. 271 days — record. Organ removed, chronic rejection</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border)">
          <td style="padding:8px">Towana Looney, 53</td>
          <td style="padding:8px">United Therapeutics</td>
          <td style="text-align:right;padding:8px">10</td>
          <td style="padding:8px">Nov 2024. 130 days. Organ removed after rejection; patient declined further immunosuppression</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border)">
          <td style="padding:8px">Bill Stewart, 54</td>
          <td style="padding:8px">eGenesis</td>
          <td style="text-align:right;padding:8px">69</td>
          <td style="padding:8px">June 2025. Off dialysis, returned to work</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border)">
          <td style="padding:8px">EXPAND Trial Patient #1</td>
          <td style="padding:8px">United Therapeutics</td>
          <td style="text-align:right;padding:8px">10</td>
          <td style="padding:8px">Nov 2025. First formal trial transplant, NYU Langone</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>Five patients. Confounding variables everywhere. Andrews was healthier at baseline than Looney. Different immunosuppression regimens. Different surgical teams. Drawing conclusions from this is bad epidemiology. But 271 days versus 130 days is a gap that researchers notice — even if the sample size makes a statistician wince.</p>

    <h2>The Trials</h2>

    <p>United Therapeutics' EXPAND trial (NCT06878560) uses a combined Phase 1/2/3 "phaseless" design. Patients with end-stage renal disease receive a UKidney and are followed for 24 weeks, then for life. Primary endpoints: patient survival, organ survival, and measured glomerular filtration rate at 24 weeks. NYU Langone is the lead site, with plans to expand to up to 30 centers.</p>

    <p>eGenesis is running a parallel trial of EGEN-2784 at Mass General, targeting patients over 50 with end-stage kidney disease who are on dialysis and awaiting transplant. Same 24-week primary assessment window. Same basic question: does the kidney work, and does it keep working?</p>

    <p>Neither trial is blinded. Neither has a control arm. There's no ethical way to randomize a dying patient to "no organ." The comparison, such as it is, will happen across trials — messy, but it's all the field has.</p>

    <h2>The Immunosuppression Tax</h2>

    <p>Here's what the optimistic coverage glosses over: every pig kidney recipient is on a punishing immunosuppression cocktail. We're talking anti-thymocyte globulin at induction, then maintenance on tacrolimus, mycophenolate, and steroids — plus experimental anti-CD154 costimulation blockers in eGenesis's protocol. That's a pharmacological siege that suppresses the entire immune system, not just the xenogeneic response.</p>

    <p>Towana Looney's case is instructive. When her kidney showed signs of rejection, she and her team decided against escalating immunosuppression further. The calculus wasn't abstract: more immune suppression means higher infection risk, higher cancer risk, kidney toxicity from the drugs themselves. For a 53-year-old woman already managing multiple conditions, the math didn't add up. She chose dialysis.</p>

    <p>The ultimate promise of xenotransplantation isn't just "pig kidneys work." It's that the genetic engineering baked into the organ eventually does enough immune evasion that you can dial back the drugs. We're not there yet. Right now, these patients are trading one medical burden (dialysis) for another (chronic immunosuppression). The question is which burden is more livable — and for how long.</p>

    <h2>The Math That Matters</h2>

    <p>More than 800,000 Americans live with end-stage kidney disease. In 2024, roughly 28,000 received human kidney transplants. That leaves about 100,000 on the active waiting list, and 17 people die every day waiting. Dialysis keeps patients alive — median survival of 5 to 10 years — at $90,000 per patient per year for Medicare. Three sessions per week, four hours each, tethered to a machine.</p>

    <p>A pig kidney doesn't need a deceased donor. It doesn't depend on a motorcycle accident or a family's grief. The source animal can be bred on demand in a pathogen-free facility. United Therapeutics operates one in Blacksburg, Virginia. eGenesis breeds its pigs with Genus PLC. Early cost estimates from a 2024 Xenotransplantation journal analysis suggest a pig organ transplant could run $200,000–$350,000 — comparable to a human transplant at roughly $440,000 in the first year but far cheaper than the cumulative $450,000+ for five years of dialysis.</p>

    <p>If either trial demonstrates 12 months of dialysis-free function with acceptable immunosuppression, the Biologics License Application that follows would reshape transplant medicine. And kidneys are just the opening act. United Therapeutics has a pig heart program. eGenesis is engineering pig livers. The organ that proves the concept unlocks the template for every other organ in the body.</p>

    <h2>The Bottom Line</h2>

    <p>Xenotransplantation spent 40 years as a punchline. The pig organs failed in minutes. Then in hours. Then in days. Andrews made it to nine months. Two FDA-approved trials are now enrolling simultaneously, and the real experiment isn't just whether pig kidneys work in humans — it's whether 10 gene edits or 69 is the right number of commits to push to a genome. The answer will take years, not months. But for the 17 people who'll die today waiting for a kidney, the race to find out started too late.</p>
  </div>

